Story location metadata: overall='medieval town'; starting='the tavern'; setting='The tavern interior is a rectangular space measuring thirty feet north–south by twenty feet east–west. Stone walls run along all four sides, and timber beams span the ceiling every five feet. The north wall hosts a single back door centered on the wall; it is three feet tall and two feet wide with a wooden latch at its center. Just inside this doorway sits a high bar stool, one foot from the northern edge of the room and half a foot east of the door’s center.\n\nAlong the east wall runs an eight‑foot long polished wooden counter that is two feet wide; it is three inches thick. At the southern end of the counter stands a hearth: a stone fireplace with an open fire pit four feet square, surrounded by a wood barrel six feet high and one foot in diameter.\n\nThe south wall contains four shelves spaced every four feet along the wall. Each shelf is two feet wide, half a foot deep, and three inches thick. On each shelf rests a crystal chalice one foot tall.\n\nThree tables line the west wall. Each table is square, two feet on each side with a polished wooden tabletop half an inch thick. The first table sits two feet from the west wall; the second and third are spaced three feet apart from each other and also two feet from the west wall. Four lanterns hang above each table, four feet above the tabletop.\n\nAt the center of the room is the hearth’s fire pit, a square four‑foot opening that provides light and warmth to the space. The bar stool near the entrance faces south toward this central hearth. All objects are positioned so they do not overlap: the counter does not touch the shelves; tables do not intersect with the hearth or the counter; lanterns hang above their respective tables without touching any other object. The back door remains accessible from the north side, and the bar stool is within easy reach of the entrance.'; location_state={'location': {'name': 'Tavern Interior', 'descrip